1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,160
They're engineered to be the strongest machines of their kind.

2
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:08,520
Whether it's hoisting 100 tons worth of jet fighters,

3
00:00:09,960 --> 00:00:14,520
toting 15 million pounds of space shuttle gear on hundreds of two-ton shoes,

4
00:00:15,220 --> 00:00:18,160
or flipping 30 acrobats to a sheer vertical,

5
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:21,520
you won't want to try any of this at home.

6
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:25,740
But if you want a bicycle that's supposed to survive a 40-foot fall,

7
00:00:25,741 --> 00:00:30,320
or a blender that'll make margaritas and pulverize your phone,

8
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:32,560
then we've got the beef.

9
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:39,240
Now, the untold story of the world's strongest on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:55,740 --> 00:01:01,972
A hundred miles off the coast of California, the U.S.

11
00:01:01,973 --> 00:01:08,440
Navy is putting on a routine test of military strength.

12
00:01:10,340 --> 00:01:16,940
The flight deck of the supercarrier USS Ronald Reagan is a fusion of jet fuel and raw nerves.

13
00:01:18,440 --> 00:01:25,360
As pilots and support personnel launch and land fighter jets around the clock,

14
00:01:25,740 --> 00:01:37,560
At maximum capacity, the Reagan carries 48 fighters, 10 support planes, and 6 helicopters.

15
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:42,220
How do they get from hangar bay to the flight deck and back again?

16
00:01:44,540 --> 00:01:47,940
Atop the world's strongest elevators ever put to sea, of course.

17
00:01:49,220 --> 00:01:52,020
They're capable of lifting 200,000 pounds.

18
00:01:52,021 --> 00:01:57,780
The other Nimitz-class carriers were actually designed with elevators that could lift 135,000 pounds.

19
00:01:58,340 --> 00:02:01,980
We actually have the strongest elevators in the world right now.

20
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:08,700
The Reagan's elevators represent 2,000 years of progress from the rope and pulley lift that Archimedes built in ancient Greece.

21
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:17,740
Although Elisha Otis built the first modern elevator with a maximum lift capacity of 4 tons in 1853,

22
00:02:17,741 --> 00:02:23,980
Today's average office elevator is rated at 3,000 pounds.

23
00:02:25,260 --> 00:02:27,640
It can lift a maximum of 15 people.

24
00:02:29,260 --> 00:02:34,180
The Reagan's heavyweight hoist can bench press the equivalent of 1,400 people,

25
00:02:34,940 --> 00:02:37,400
three stories, in about 10 seconds.

26
00:02:38,940 --> 00:02:41,960
The carrier is equipped with four of these monster lifters,

27
00:02:42,740 --> 00:02:44,940
located along the edges of the carrier's flight deck,

28
00:02:44,941 --> 00:02:48,940
One on the port side and three on the starboard side.

29
00:02:48,941 --> 00:02:54,940
From the flight deck, they descend 40 feet to access the hangar bay,

30
00:02:54,941 --> 00:02:59,940
essentially the world's largest floating repair and storage facility.

31
00:03:03,940 --> 00:03:06,940
Our hangar bays is pretty much the garage.

32
00:03:06,941 --> 00:03:11,940
The aircraft, after it goes out and does its mission, may need to change the tires,

33
00:03:11,941 --> 00:03:13,440
may need to change an engine.

34
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:16,540
So we've got to get that aircraft from the flight deck

35
00:03:16,541 --> 00:03:18,440
down to the hangar bay

36
00:03:18,441 --> 00:03:22,940
so the maintenance support can go do repairs to it.

37
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:29,800
A typical elevator run up to the flight deck

38
00:03:29,801 --> 00:03:33,300
includes two fully loaded F-A-18 fighters

39
00:03:33,301 --> 00:03:37,080
for a grand total of 132,000 pounds.

40
00:03:38,580 --> 00:03:41,500
That's 68,000 pounds short of its capacity.

41
00:03:41,940 --> 00:03:48,940
Engineers built the Reagan to serve for 50 years, so the extra muscle could come in handy in the years to come.

42
00:03:48,941 --> 00:03:53,940
The next generation fighter, we don't know how much it weighs right now,

43
00:03:53,941 --> 00:03:59,940
so we've got to have the capability to support that strike fighter, hard bomber or whatever in the future.

44
00:03:59,941 --> 00:04:03,940
Okay, who in the hangar bay is telling people that there is no elevator run?

45
00:04:03,941 --> 00:04:07,940
We're going to put 612 on there. I'm sending you 610.

46
00:04:07,941 --> 00:04:11,960
When the flight deck handler orders two fully loaded F-A-18s,

47
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:15,340
the crew hustles out the fighters and chains them to the elevator.

48
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:23,080
To ensure absolute safety, two operators, one in the hangar and one on the flight deck,

49
00:04:23,740 --> 00:04:28,220
must simultaneously turn their switches to engage the quarter-million pound slabs.

50
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:32,840
Twenty thick steel cables do the heavy lifting.

51
00:04:32,841 --> 00:04:42,480
Each individual cable is so strong that 19 of them could break, and the one remaining cable could lift the loaded elevator.

52
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:48,200
But the real muscle that powers these elevators is hidden below decks.

53
00:04:50,420 --> 00:04:58,220
In the pump room, load centers convert electric power from the ship's two nuclear reactors into pure hydraulic adrenaline.

54
00:04:58,221 --> 00:05:04,820
The load center provides the power for the hydraulic pumps to either raise or lower the

55
00:05:04,821 --> 00:05:07,400
elevators through the use of electric hydraulic force.

56
00:05:07,401 --> 00:05:13,200
What we're looking at right here is the exhaust tank, the main start of our hydraulic fluid

57
00:05:13,201 --> 00:05:14,200
path.

58
00:05:14,201 --> 00:05:15,600
Maximum capacity is 2,500 gallons.

59
00:05:15,601 --> 00:05:20,020
As you can see, it goes up from the third deck all the way up almost to the first deck

60
00:05:20,021 --> 00:05:21,020
of the hangar base.

61
00:05:21,021 --> 00:05:25,340
So we're talking almost 16 feet tall, about 6 feet round.

62
00:05:25,341 --> 00:05:34,100
fluid comes out of it through the six inch pipe right here into our four main pumps down here in

63
00:05:34,101 --> 00:05:35,528
the machinery room.

64
00:05:35,529 --> 00:05:41,240
The four pumps push hydraulic fluid into a bank of 21 pressure storage units

65
00:05:41,241 --> 00:05:42,928
called accumulators.

66
00:05:42,929 --> 00:05:48,920
When the elevator engages, the pressurized fluid powers the main engine

67
00:05:48,921 --> 00:05:49,621
or RAM.

68
00:05:49,622 --> 00:05:55,920
The RAM travels along a 26-inch diameter cylinder, pulling the steel cables and lifting

69
00:05:56,700 --> 00:05:57,746
the elevator.

70
00:05:57,747 --> 00:06:03,700
The 8-ton RAM pulls the cables with a force equal to 5,700 horsepower, the

71
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:08,122
equivalent of 12 big rigs.

72
00:06:08,123 --> 00:06:13,100
These power lifters are plenty impressive, but consider how much

73
00:06:13,101 --> 00:06:21,400
But consider how much strength it takes to jack up NASA's space shuttle and move it to the launch pad.

74
00:06:31,140 --> 00:06:37,840
For that, you'll need the world's strongest self-propelled flatback, the Crawler Transporter.

75
00:06:39,340 --> 00:06:42,740
NASA's shuttle taxi cab weighs a svelte 6 million pounds.

76
00:06:43,100 --> 00:06:46,100
with a roof about the size of a baseball diamond.

77
00:06:48,120 --> 00:06:51,760
Its passenger waits in the Vehicle Assembly Building, or VAB.

78
00:06:54,660 --> 00:06:57,620
Technicians inside this 52-story space garage

79
00:06:57,621 --> 00:06:59,740
give the shuttle its final tune-up.

80
00:07:03,160 --> 00:07:05,660
The shuttle and its companion rocket boosters

81
00:07:05,661 --> 00:07:07,660
sit atop the mobile launch platform,

82
00:07:07,980 --> 00:07:10,380
which weighs 9 million pounds.

83
00:07:13,100 --> 00:07:19,880
To get these behemoths off the ground, the crawler transporter uses some seriously strong hydraulic muscle.

84
00:07:22,260 --> 00:07:27,200
The system is called GEL, for jacking, equalization, and leveling.

85
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:30,000
Basically, an enormous floor jack.

86
00:07:31,180 --> 00:07:35,880
The actual load of the mobile launcher and shuttle elements are assumed by four small points,

87
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:40,980
one directly above the crawler truck assembly on all four of the corners of the chassis.

88
00:07:43,100 --> 00:07:53,560
After the crawler moves beneath the mobile launcher platform,

89
00:07:54,520 --> 00:07:59,480
the gel's 16 20-inch diameter cylinders jack the structure 18 inches,

90
00:07:59,960 --> 00:08:04,000
under 3,300 pounds per square inch of hydraulic pressure.

91
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:07,160
That operation's fairly delicate.

92
00:08:07,760 --> 00:08:11,440
From a vertical clearance perspective, we only have 2 to 3 inches of clearance

93
00:08:11,441 --> 00:08:13,600
from the bottom of the launcher to the top of the crawler.

94
00:08:14,600 --> 00:08:16,500
When the fragile docking is complete,

95
00:08:17,020 --> 00:08:18,320
the crawler hits reverse

96
00:08:18,321 --> 00:08:22,520
and begins the long six-hour trek to the launch pad four miles distant.

97
00:08:25,360 --> 00:08:28,180
Moving a combined weight of 18 million pounds

98
00:08:28,181 --> 00:08:32,140
is done at a top speed of 0.9 miles per hour.

99
00:08:33,180 --> 00:08:34,220
A snail's pace.

100
00:08:35,100 --> 00:08:37,880
But inching along on 456 shoes,

101
00:08:38,520 --> 00:08:40,760
the crawler is more like a mutant centipede.

102
00:08:41,440 --> 00:08:44,140
We're walking alongside one of the eight crawler tread belts.

103
00:08:44,700 --> 00:08:47,620
Each tread belt is comprised of 57 individual shoes.

104
00:08:48,420 --> 00:08:51,220
Each of the shoes weighs approximately 2,000 pounds.

105
00:08:51,221 --> 00:08:57,080
The lugs that are holding the adjacent shoes together each weigh approximately 80 to 100 pounds.

106
00:08:58,360 --> 00:09:01,180
King Kong footwear demands good hydraulic hygiene.

107
00:09:02,160 --> 00:09:05,520
NASA's method for preventing athlete's foot is a gooey mess.

108
00:09:06,640 --> 00:09:11,140
What we're looking at is one track on one truck of the crawler transporter.

109
00:09:11,440 --> 00:09:13,320
As you can see, grease is applied liberally.

110
00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:14,980
This is due to the environment that we're in.

111
00:09:14,981 --> 00:09:18,980
As you can see with the crawler rock, the dust, the grit and the grime gets up inside here.

112
00:09:20,540 --> 00:09:23,000
The contamination can cause crankshafting,

113
00:09:23,620 --> 00:09:26,980
a condition that wears down the shoes and elongates the tread belt.

114
00:09:27,980 --> 00:09:31,360
We've gotten around this by installing this belt pin lubrication system.

115
00:09:31,680 --> 00:09:36,480
These are timed by the PLC to shoot belt pin lube in between the shoes themselves,

116
00:09:36,680 --> 00:09:39,980
bring down the level of crankshafting and prolong the life of the belt.

117
00:09:39,981 --> 00:09:46,260
Despite their cast-steel alloy construction, these shoes are not built for cross-country.

118
00:09:47,620 --> 00:09:52,400
Their performance depends on a cushy track that can support a multi-million pound load.

119
00:09:53,620 --> 00:09:57,080
The base of the crawlerway starts with four feet of sand,

120
00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:01,200
another four feet of heavily compacted limestone rock,

121
00:10:02,020 --> 00:10:04,520
and finally four to eight inches of river gravel.

122
00:10:06,040 --> 00:10:09,740
The soft gravel cushions the crawler tracks and minimizes friction.

123
00:10:09,980 --> 00:10:14,980
They are the undisputed world's strongest load-bearing tracks.

124
00:10:14,981 --> 00:10:18,980
But the muscle behind this monster truck is under the hood.

125
00:10:18,981 --> 00:10:21,980
This is one of our two DC propel engines on board the crawler.

126
00:10:21,981 --> 00:10:24,980
It's an ALCO 2750 horsepower engine.

127
00:10:24,981 --> 00:10:27,980
Both of them are operating concurrently when the crawler is operating in normal configuration.

128
00:10:27,981 --> 00:10:31,980
And each one turns a shaft supplying power to these thousand kilowatt generators.

129
00:10:31,981 --> 00:10:35,980
And they're responsible for supplying power to the 16 propel motors that are on corners of the crawler.

130
00:10:35,981 --> 00:10:38,980
They're responsible for taking the crawler down the crawler line.

131
00:10:38,981 --> 00:10:46,500
That's a total of 5,500 horsepower, the same that is produced by 13 Corvettes.

132
00:10:47,780 --> 00:10:57,260
There's power to burn, but the real secret to the world's strongest crawler is how it converts that power to the phenomenal torque needed to move millions of pounds.

133
00:10:59,960 --> 00:11:03,000
Each of the eight tread belts uses two propel motors.

134
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:08,420
Each motor turns a shaft. The power is a five-stage reduction gear train.

135
00:11:08,980 --> 00:11:15,980
Every 168 revolutions of the motor shaft equates to one turn of the propel sprocket.

136
00:11:15,981 --> 00:11:19,980
The result is overwhelming torque multiplication.

137
00:11:19,981 --> 00:11:24,980
While high-speed cornering and acceleration aren't of much concern,

138
00:11:24,981 --> 00:11:26,980
steering is.

139
00:11:26,981 --> 00:11:30,980
Two drivers, one in the front and one at the rear,

140
00:11:30,981 --> 00:11:35,980
control four hydraulic steering cylinders located above each track assembly.

141
00:11:35,981 --> 00:11:43,760
With the assistance of several spotters on the ground, the drivers make minute steering adjustments that keep the crawler on course.

142
00:11:44,900 --> 00:11:49,240
These are actually in degrees, and what we're in right now is called great circle steering.

143
00:11:49,720 --> 00:11:52,800
It's where the front trucks turn this way and the back trucks turn this way and follow an arc.

144
00:11:53,480 --> 00:11:57,220
We also have crab, which allows us to move diagonal.

145
00:11:58,340 --> 00:12:04,740
The drivers keep the crawler on target, but it takes an onboard crew of four engineers and a dozen technicians

146
00:12:04,741 --> 00:12:07,520
to keep the precious cargo atop it safe.

147
00:12:09,100 --> 00:12:12,900
Monitoring everything from engines and pumps to the most critical task.

148
00:12:14,120 --> 00:12:16,360
Keeping the shuttle rock steady.

149
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:23,480
The 16 gel cylinders are adjusted to keep the 3 million pound shuttle elements perfectly level.

150
00:12:26,120 --> 00:12:31,220
While eight 28 inch long bolts guarantee that nothing slips from the platform.

151
00:12:31,221 --> 00:12:39,800
At the launch pad, the crawler lowers the mobile launch platform into place and backs away.

152
00:12:41,860 --> 00:12:44,840
It's a method perfected with decades of practice.

153
00:12:46,700 --> 00:12:54,560
NASA's two crawlers have traveled the equivalent of 3,800 miles, or the distance between Cape Canaveral and Alaska.

154
00:12:56,800 --> 00:13:00,580
The Marion Power Shovel Company built the crawler in 1964.

155
00:13:01,220 --> 00:13:06,940
when NASA decided on a mobile launcher for the Apollo program's massive Saturn V.

156
00:13:07,980 --> 00:13:11,900
At 8 million pounds, the heaviest rocket ever launched.

157
00:13:15,040 --> 00:13:19,000
Early in the test phase, the space program came to a screeching halt

158
00:13:19,001 --> 00:13:22,140
when crews discovered metal fragments on the crawler way.

159
00:13:23,040 --> 00:13:25,300
They had come from the crawler track bearings,

160
00:13:25,700 --> 00:13:28,000
which were unable to support the tremendous weight.

161
00:13:28,001 --> 00:13:33,820
After months of scathing media attacks and a lot of re-engineering,

162
00:13:34,920 --> 00:13:38,260
Marion and NASA engineers put the moon missions back on track.

163
00:13:39,760 --> 00:13:44,020
We now have bronze alloy sleeve bushings in those main rollers,

164
00:13:44,260 --> 00:13:46,040
and they've served us quite well to this day.

165
00:13:46,440 --> 00:13:51,660
But at one time, that problem was a real potential showstopper for the start of the entire program.

166
00:13:52,580 --> 00:13:55,840
The show went on, and after the Apollo program,

167
00:13:55,841 --> 00:14:00,160
its successors, and more than 128 shuttle missions.

168
00:14:01,160 --> 00:14:07,520
The crawler-transporter is now poised to shoulder the load of NASA's upcoming Constellation space program.

169
00:14:09,080 --> 00:14:12,560
Its powerful shoes will haul whatever NASA can dream up.

170
00:14:13,460 --> 00:14:18,460
But when there's a need for speed, shoes just don't cut it in a coal mine.

171
00:14:19,300 --> 00:14:24,840
The world's strongest tire rules this dig, and you won't believe the jello mold it came from.

172
00:14:25,840 --> 00:14:32,680
During launch rollout, the crawler transporter keeps its load level to such a degree that

173
00:14:32,681 --> 00:14:39,580
the nose cone of the space shuttle does not sway outside the diameter of a basketball.

174
00:14:39,581 --> 00:14:44,520
World's Strongest 3 will return on Modern Marvels.

175
00:14:44,521 --> 00:14:50,080
We now return to World's Strongest 3 on Modern Marvels.

176
00:14:55,840 --> 00:15:04,400
In the depths of this open-pit coal mine in Wyoming's Powder River Basin, everything

177
00:15:04,401 --> 00:15:10,080
is big, really big, including this LeTorno L2350.

178
00:15:10,081 --> 00:15:17,280
It can excavate and load 100,000 tons of material in a day's work.

179
00:15:17,281 --> 00:15:21,920
To get the job done, it relies on the world's strongest tires.

180
00:15:21,921 --> 00:15:27,920
As each successive technology has increased, both the tire technology and equipment technology,

181
00:15:27,921 --> 00:15:30,920
the sizes continue to get bigger and bigger and bigger.

182
00:15:30,921 --> 00:15:35,920
It's all about productivity, moving more material faster, more efficiently,

183
00:15:35,921 --> 00:15:39,920
to be able to complete the projects, bring them to fruition sooner.

184
00:15:39,921 --> 00:15:44,920
At the Bridgestone Firestone off-road tire plant in Bloomington Normal, Illinois,

185
00:15:44,921 --> 00:15:49,920
the world's strongest tire is born amidst an ocean of rubber.

186
00:15:49,921 --> 00:15:54,940
The three tires that you're looking at right here represent the output of our sister plants.

187
00:15:55,040 --> 00:15:58,040
These are the tires that most of the consumers are familiar with.

188
00:15:58,100 --> 00:16:01,680
A sports car tire, a passenger tire, and a light pickup truck tire.

189
00:16:02,080 --> 00:16:08,060
What I have here represents the output of the Bloomington Normal plant from 4 foot to 6 foot to 8 foot to 10 foot,

190
00:16:08,400 --> 00:16:11,520
all the way up to the 13 foot world's strongest tire.

191
00:16:11,521 --> 00:16:25,521
The Bridgestone 7070-57 Super Rock Grip Deep Tread Tire weighs about the same as a school bus, nearly 15,000 pounds, with a load-carrying capacity of 300,000 pounds.

192
00:16:29,120 --> 00:16:35,280
Bridgestone Firestone constructs the 7070 in layers, or plies, of rubberized nylon cords.

193
00:16:35,281 --> 00:16:43,100
The nylon provides strength to the rubber, and the rubber insulates the nylon cords to prevent them from rubbing against each other.

194
00:16:44,280 --> 00:16:51,260
One finished tire contains 48 plies, more than enough surface area to cover an Olympic-sized swimming pool.

195
00:16:53,820 --> 00:17:02,020
Each tire requires 40 workers and 88 hours of painstaking labor, and a mountain of raw rubber.

196
00:17:05,280 --> 00:17:09,740
The first step is to join the nylon cords to form the rubber plies.

197
00:17:10,380 --> 00:17:17,320
Each of these plies is a rubberized nylon, nylon cord very much like I have in my hand right here.

198
00:17:17,580 --> 00:17:21,580
There's up to 21 of these cords in every inch of that ply.

199
00:17:22,120 --> 00:17:24,640
The plies are up to 17 foot wide.

200
00:17:25,540 --> 00:17:30,560
A team of skilled tire builders then begins layering the 48 plies on a massive hub.

201
00:17:30,561 --> 00:17:37,460
There's a combination of high technology that goes into building the tire and a fascinating

202
00:17:37,461 --> 00:17:41,840
amount of hand craftsmanship that has to happen in order to get the tires produced.

203
00:17:42,200 --> 00:17:45,240
A passenger tire, when we build it, takes about six minutes.

204
00:17:45,740 --> 00:17:49,700
It takes these four men 14 hours to put together one tire.

205
00:17:50,520 --> 00:17:53,480
Every 12th ply, the workers insert a bead.

206
00:17:55,780 --> 00:17:58,260
The bead is like a heavy steel hula hoop.

207
00:17:58,261 --> 00:18:03,260
Its high tensile strength keeps the tire mounted on the wheel.

208
00:18:03,261 --> 00:18:07,260
One tire requires eight beads.

209
00:18:07,261 --> 00:18:12,260
What I have in front of me here is one complete set of beads for a 70-70-57.

210
00:18:12,261 --> 00:18:19,260
These eight beads, each of them weighing 80 pounds, combined total of 640 pounds of steel here,

211
00:18:19,261 --> 00:18:26,260
is comprised of 20 individual strands of wire insulated with a special rubber compound

212
00:18:26,261 --> 00:18:28,420
to keep the wires from abrading against each other.

213
00:18:28,880 --> 00:18:34,700
These 20 strands have been wrapped around a 57-inch diameter wheel 44 times.

214
00:18:35,620 --> 00:18:40,740
The amount of wire, if I laid it out end-to-end, would stretch out nearly 20 miles in total length.

215
00:18:41,860 --> 00:18:44,780
Once the beads and all 48 plies are in place,

216
00:18:45,460 --> 00:18:49,060
a stitching machine removes the trapped air and seals up the sections.

217
00:18:51,040 --> 00:18:54,960
Workers then place the hulking rubber roll on a hydraulic press for shaping.

218
00:18:56,260 --> 00:19:02,420
The press applies pressure on the outside, while a huge rubber bladder inflates on the

219
00:19:02,421 --> 00:19:07,260
inside. And a tire begins to take shape.

220
00:19:07,261 --> 00:19:12,700
We're inflating that bladder with carbon dioxide at about 25 pounds of pressure, while

221
00:19:12,701 --> 00:19:15,247
at the same time we're compressing the beads together.

222
00:19:15,248 --> 00:19:17,180
So we're taking the tire from essentially

223
00:19:17,181 --> 00:19:21,020
a barrel shape down into a tire shape.

224
00:19:21,021 --> 00:19:24,340
But this tire has only just begun to cook.

225
00:19:25,500 --> 00:19:27,560
Workers next place it into a large mold.

226
00:19:28,660 --> 00:19:33,780
A 105-ton domed oven filled with hot steam covers the mold.

227
00:19:35,120 --> 00:19:37,880
Scalding water fills the rubber bladder inside the tire.

228
00:19:39,560 --> 00:19:42,920
We're heating the tire from the inside out and the outside in simultaneously.

229
00:19:43,520 --> 00:19:45,640
And rubber's not a very good conductor of heat,

230
00:19:45,641 --> 00:19:50,800
so it takes about 22 hours to get this tire up to the temperatures on the very deepest inside portions.

231
00:19:51,020 --> 00:19:53,240
So now is when the chemical reaction takes place.

232
00:19:53,860 --> 00:19:56,260
The chemical reaction is called vulcanization.

233
00:19:57,400 --> 00:20:01,040
Heat causes the rubber molecules to physically link with sulfur molecules,

234
00:20:01,860 --> 00:20:04,340
producing a more stable and durable material.

235
00:20:06,660 --> 00:20:09,280
The mold's shape forms the tire's tread pattern.

236
00:20:10,580 --> 00:20:13,920
At this stage of the process, we're nearly finished with the tire.

237
00:20:14,040 --> 00:20:15,220
This is final inspection.

238
00:20:15,940 --> 00:20:18,280
What the worker here is going to do is he's going to examine

239
00:20:18,281 --> 00:20:23,680
every single square inch of this tire all around the outside and all around the inside.

240
00:20:24,200 --> 00:20:27,900
If he finds a little molding anomaly, he'll address it and take care of it.

241
00:20:28,800 --> 00:20:35,260
Before the tire hits the dirt, designers have to be certain it can support massive loads over months of service.

242
00:20:36,580 --> 00:20:41,440
Each tire is structurally over-designed to ensure that a rapid air pressure loss,

243
00:20:41,920 --> 00:20:45,320
or the world's biggest blowout, can never happen.

244
00:20:45,321 --> 00:20:50,320
We're going to put 100 pounds of pressure in the tire, which is its normal operating conditions,

245
00:20:50,321 --> 00:20:57,320
and then we're going to load the tire against this 15-foot diameter test drum at 250,000 pounds of pressure.

246
00:20:57,321 --> 00:21:01,320
We're going to measure the deflection, we're going to measure the temperature in the tire,

247
00:21:01,321 --> 00:21:07,320
and all the parameters that the tire has to live up to to deliver the expectations that our customers want.

248
00:21:07,321 --> 00:21:12,320
There's one and only one vehicle using the 7070 off-road tire.

249
00:21:12,321 --> 00:21:19,520
today it's pulling a double shift removing rock and soil in a wyoming coal mine

250
00:21:19,521 --> 00:21:25,620
the latorno l2350 is the world's strongest wheeled loader

251
00:21:25,621 --> 00:21:34,540
it's no job for a first-time driver starting to come into the bank hoisting

252
00:21:34,541 --> 00:21:41,980
curl in the bucket as i go through usually it takes about four or five buckets to fill the truck

253
00:21:42,320 --> 00:21:46,940
That's about 400 tons of dirt to fill the biggest haul truck.

254
00:21:50,500 --> 00:21:54,840
Since the tires are so critical to productivity, they need protection.

255
00:21:56,220 --> 00:22:01,100
On around here, we have a set of chains installed on these tires.

256
00:22:01,260 --> 00:22:05,020
The purpose of these chains is to avoid damage to these tires.

257
00:22:05,340 --> 00:22:08,100
These chains weigh approximately 5 to 6 tons.

258
00:22:08,460 --> 00:22:10,640
They run only on the front of the loader tires.

259
00:22:12,320 --> 00:22:17,100
They keep it so you can't get rocks or anything in to cut any of the tread.

260
00:22:17,240 --> 00:22:20,540
Once the tread gets cut, get a little moisture in it, it separates.

261
00:22:21,060 --> 00:22:23,020
Then the tire is no longer of any use to us.

262
00:22:25,340 --> 00:22:32,200
In the Powder River coal mines, a set of four hard-working 7070 tires will last about six months.

263
00:22:33,760 --> 00:22:38,780
They'll do for now, until the day that new compounds or construction techniques

264
00:22:38,781 --> 00:22:42,780
crown the next world's strongest tire and its companion loader.

265
00:22:44,780 --> 00:22:48,320
A strong tire may be the key to mining on a massive scale.

266
00:22:50,320 --> 00:22:52,600
But for a small number of daredevils,

267
00:22:53,180 --> 00:22:56,040
it's not the tires, but the frame that really matters

268
00:22:56,041 --> 00:23:00,220
when trying to survive a jump on the world's strongest bike.

